.\" head.1 -- maux's head(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (V7 had no head; it is a BSD addition), so it carries no
.\" third-party notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as
.\" more.1. Rendered at build time to build/man/HEAD.TXT and
.\" staged on the play image as /usr/man/HEAD: `more
.\" /usr/man/HEAD`.
.TH HEAD 1 "September 13, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
head \- print the first few lines of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B head
[
.BI \-n " number"
|
.BI \- number
] [ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Head
copies the first
.I number
lines of each
.I file
(standard input default) to the standard output.
.I Number
defaults to 10.
.BI \-n " number"
and the older
.BI \- number
form are equivalent.
.PP
With more than one
.I file
argument, the output of each is preceded by a header of the
form
.IP
.B "==>" " name" " <=="
.PP
with one blank line before each header but the first.
Exactly one operand, or none, never gets a header.
.PP
.I Head
is a straight prefix copy: it stops the moment
.I number
lines have gone by, so a file's own missing final newline is
carried through unchanged rather than supplied.
A
.I number
larger than a file's line count copies the whole file; this is
not an error.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/HEAD  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
tail(1), cat(1), more(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0, or 1 when a file named on the command line
could not be opened -- that file is skipped, a diagnostic
naming it is written to standard error, and the remaining
operands are still processed.
.SH BUGS
.BI \- number
and
.BI \-n " number"
accept only decimal digits; anything else is a usage error, exit
2.
